title: Before writing a rule about existing behavior, read the code path that decides it
pid: 6ae8ee24-6e92-4f9d-8aec-12ef8104af5c
body: |2-
  A plan that says "X happens when Y" is a claim about the code. Three
  such claims in the TDV effort were false, and each cost a round: that
  the graph pair identifies a text preserver (it does not; a title
  heuristic was built and then deleted), that the editable map
  write-protects a later occurrence so editability cannot move (only an
  editable occurrence claims its ID, so it can), and that a request on
  an editable node reaches the cascade (two save rules refused it; the
  run stopped). The habit: find the function that decides the behavior,
  read it, and cite it in the plan. If you cannot find it, say so in the
  plan as an open question rather than stating the rule.

  The rule binds the planner's own sentences as much as the plan's
  rules. In plan 5 the planner wrote that a save re-sent after it
  created a child is not refused, without tracing it; the executing
  agent tested it and found the opposite.

  A rule that two copies of a thing are equal must say in which
  canonical form they are compared. Plan 2_2 said a graphnode the
  buffer shows "as the graph has it" never refuses a save, and
  compared raw ID lists; a graph's content list can name one graphnode
  twice (an extra ID after a nodeMerge) where a view draws it once, so
  every save of a view of such a container was refused until a probe
  found it.
